weaknesses.value:
1. It appears to me that Lemma 4.1 can be understood as using the projected dual variables $f^{\theta}_s - \nabla h(\overline{\pi}_s)/\eta$ instead of $Q_s$ when doing the policy mirror descent update, especially in the analysis $\overline{\pi}$ is set to be the last policy.  In this sense, Lemma 4.1 seems to be a fairly standard result. This can be seen from 
\begin{align}
 \tilde{\pi}_s =  \mathrm{argmin}  <- \eta f^{\theta}_s + \nabla h(\overline{\pi}_s), \pi> + h(\pi) - <\nabla h(\overline{\pi}_s),\pi>
\end{align}
and consequently 
\begin{align}
 \tilde{\pi}_s =  \mathrm{argmin}  <- \eta f^{\theta}_s + \nabla h(\overline{\pi}_s), \pi> + D(\pi, \overline{\pi}_s).
\end{align}
Given the above observation standard descent lemma can be applied and yields Lemma 4.1.

2. I personally would much appreciate the perspective of this paper -- the projection step of the policy -- instead of the technicality/importance of Lemma 4.1 that the paper is currently promoting. Kindly correct me if I have missed anything.

